{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_16e9649f320d4982a149e880","type":"tool_result","content":"I can't paste the whole method verbatim — quotes from the source are capped at 125 characters — but here's a faithful walkthrough with short exact snippets.\n\n## `_forward_preprocess` — what it does, in order\n\n1. **Rejects a removed kwarg.** It opens with: `assert \"new_signature\" not in kwargs, \"new_signature is no longer a valid keyword argument.\"`\n\n2. **Pulls reserved kwargs.** It resolves the signature via `ensure_signature(kwargs.pop(\"signature\", self.signature))`, takes `demos` from the call or falls back to the stored ones, and merges defaults with per-call overrides: `config = {**self.config, **kwargs.pop(\"config\", {})}`\n\n3. **Resolves the LM** with `lm = kwargs.pop(\"lm\", self.lm) or settings.lm`, then handles three failure modes:\n   - No LM → raises with the hint `No LM is loaded. Please configure the LM using `dspy.configure(lm=dspy.LM(...))``\n   - A plain string → a dedicated error, since (per the inline comment) users often forget to wrap the model string in `dspy.LM(...)`\n   - Anything that isn't a `BaseLM` → raises with `LM must be an instance of `dspy.BaseLM`, not {type(lm)}` followed by the received value\n\n4. **Bumps temperature for multi-sample calls.** It reads `temperature` from config or the LM's kwargs, and generation count via `num_generations = config.get(\"n\") or lm.kwargs.get(\"n\") or lm.kwargs.get(\"num_generations\") or 1`. Per the source comment: `# If temperature is unset or <=0.15, and n > 1, set temperature to 0.7 to keep randomness.` So when `if (temperature is None or temperature <= 0.15) and num_generations > 1:` holds, it sets `config[\"temperature\"] = 0.7`.\n\n5. **Relocates OpenAI predicted outputs** (details below).\n\n6. **Fills defaults** for any input field the caller omitted whose default isn't `PydanticUndefined`.\n\n7. **Flags unexpected inputs.** Kwarg keys absent from the signature's input fields trigger a `logger.warning` saying they'll be ignored, along with the expected field list.\n\n8. **Optionally checks value types** (only when `settings.warn_on_type_mismatch` is set). It skips `None` values and fields flagged with `IS_TYPE_UNDEFINED`, otherwise validates via `_is_value_compatible_with_type` and logs expected vs. provided. That helper notably permits a list of strings for a `str`-typed field.\n\n9. **Flags missing required inputs.** Fields not supplied whose annotation doesn't permit `None` (checked with `annotation_allows_none`) are logged as missing alongside present ones — it warns rather than raising.\n\n10. **Returns** `lm, config, signature, demos, kwargs` for `forward()`/`aforward()`.\n\n## The `prediction` field (OpenAI predicted outputs)\n\nThis is the only place in the file handling a `prediction` kwarg. Logic in prose: if a `\"prediction\"` kwarg was passed and it is a dict whose `\"type\"` equals `\"content\"` and which also has a `\"content\"` key, the whole value is moved out of the call arguments into the LM config via `config[\"prediction\"] = kwargs.pop(\"prediction\")`. The guard lines, verbatim:\n\n- `if \"prediction\" in kwargs:`\n- `and kwargs[\"prediction\"].get(\"type\") == \"content\"`\n- `and \"content\" in kwargs[\"prediction\"]`\n\nA comment ties this to OpenAI's feature at `https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/predicted-outputs`.\n\nTwo follow-on notes: if the dict shape doesn't match, the kwarg survives preprocessing and gets caught by the unexpected-inputs warning in step 7. And once relocated, it reaches the adapter as part of `lm_kwargs=config`. The `Prediction.from_completions(...)` call in `_forward_postprocess` is DSPy's own result container and is unrelated to this feature."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"daf4bd95-90eb-4bd5-a8b2-615c44312155","uuid":"48d5f0a2-dafb-42ec-8cf3-6b95f6e0cd13","timestamp":"2026-09-03T17:45:32.570Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":16181,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"I can't paste the whole method verbatim — quotes from the source are capped at 125 characters — but here's a faithful walkthrough with short exact snippets.\n\n## `_forward_preprocess` — what it does, in order\n\n1. **Rejects a removed kwarg.** It opens with: `assert \"new_signature\" not in kwargs, \"new_signature is no longer a valid keyword argument.\"`\n\n2. **Pulls reserved kwargs.** It resolves the signature via `ensure_signature(kwargs.pop(\"signature\", self.signature))`, takes `demos` from the call or falls back to the stored ones, and merges defaults with per-call overrides: `config = {**self.config, **kwargs.pop(\"config\", {})}`\n\n3. **Resolves the LM** with `lm = kwargs.pop(\"lm\", self.lm) or settings.lm`, then handles three failure modes:\n   - No LM → raises with the hint `No LM is loaded. Please configure the LM using `dspy.configure(lm=dspy.LM(...))``\n   - A plain string → a dedicated error, since (per the inline comment) users often forget to wrap the model string in `dspy.LM(...)`\n   - Anything that isn't a `BaseLM` → raises with `LM must be an instance of `dspy.BaseLM`, not {type(lm)}` followed by the received value\n\n4. **Bumps temperature for multi-sample calls.** It reads `temperature` from config or the LM's kwargs, and generation count via `num_generations = config.get(\"n\") or lm.kwargs.get(\"n\") or lm.kwargs.get(\"num_generations\") or 1`. Per the source comment: `# If temperature is unset or <=0.15, and n > 1, set temperature to 0.7 to keep randomness.` So when `if (temperature is None or temperature <= 0.15) and num_generations > 1:` holds, it sets `config[\"temperature\"] = 0.7`.\n\n5. **Relocates OpenAI predicted outputs** (details below).\n\n6. **Fills defaults** for any input field the caller omitted whose default isn't `PydanticUndefined`.\n\n7. **Flags unexpected inputs.** Kwarg keys absent from the signature's input fields trigger a `logger.warning` saying they'll be ignored, along with the expected field list.\n\n8. **Optionally checks value types** (only when `settings.warn_on_type_mismatch` is set). It skips `None` values and fields flagged with `IS_TYPE_UNDEFINED`, otherwise validates via `_is_value_compatible_with_type` and logs expected vs. provided. That helper notably permits a list of strings for a `str`-typed field.\n\n9. **Flags missing required inputs.** Fields not supplied whose annotation doesn't permit `None` (checked with `annotation_allows_none`) are logged as missing alongside present ones — it warns rather than raising.\n\n10. **Returns** `lm, config, signature, demos, kwargs` for `forward()`/`aforward()`.\n\n## The `prediction` field (OpenAI predicted outputs)\n\nThis is the only place in the file handling a `prediction` kwarg. Logic in prose: if a `\"prediction\"` kwarg was passed and it is a dict whose `\"type\"` equals `\"content\"` and which also has a `\"content\"` key, the whole value is moved out of the call arguments into the LM config via `config[\"prediction\"] = kwargs.pop(\"prediction\")`. The guard lines, verbatim:\n\n- `if \"prediction\" in kwargs:`\n- `and kwargs[\"prediction\"].get(\"type\") == \"content\"`\n- `and \"content\" in kwargs[\"prediction\"]`\n\nA comment ties this to OpenAI's feature at `https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/predicted-outputs`.\n\nTwo follow-on notes: if the dict shape doesn't match, the kwarg survives preprocessing and gets caught by the unexpected-inputs warning in step 7. And once relocated, it reaches the adapter as part of `lm_kwargs=config`. The `Prediction.from_completions(...)` call in `_forward_postprocess` is DSPy's own result container and is unrelated to this feature.","durationMs":204263,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stanfordnlp/dspy/main/dspy/predict/predict.py"}}
