verrides forward. Fine.\n\nAlso consider: does this need handling for other modules, e.g., `dspy.ChainOfThought` (subclasses Predict → inherits forward). Good.\n\nAlso, what about the config merging: `config = dict(**self.config, **kwargs.pop(\"config\", {}))`. If the user passes `config={\"prediction\": ...}` explicitly, that also works already. Adding to config after that means the kwargs-derived prediction would override... Actually order: I set `config[\"prediction\"] = ...` after, so it overrides any explicit config prediction. Upstream does the same. Fine.\n\nNow — does `prediction` in the LM kwargs reach litellm properly? Yes, `kwargs = {**self.kwargs, **kwargs}` in `LM.forward`, then `request=dict(model=..., messages=..., **kwargs)` → `litellm.completion(**request)`. litellm supports the `prediction` param for OpenAI.\n\nAlso the cache key includes the full request, so different predictions get different cache entries. Good.\n\nLet me now write the change and add tests. Tests to add in `tests/predict/test_predict.py`:\n\n1. `test_prediction_field_passed_as_config`: SpyLM records kwargs; call `program(question=..., prediction={\"type\": \"content\", \"content\": \"Paris is...\"})`; assert `lm.calls[0][\"kwargs\"][\"prediction\"] == {...}`.\n2. `test_prediction_field_with_invalid_format_treated_as_input`: signature with a `prediction` input field; passing a non-conforming dict keeps it as input.\n3. Maybe test that a valid-format prediction is removed from messages (not rendered in the user message).\n4. Async test.\n\nLet me check `DummyLM` in `dspy/utils/dummies.py` to see if it accepts kwargs — but for testing config passing, SpyLM pattern is better.\n\nAlso check whether there's a `tests/predict/test_predict.py` style for the async test — there is `test_async_predict`.\n\nNow write the code. Also, should the check require `isinstance(kwargs[\"prediction\"], dict)`? The user said \"it should be a dictionary with a 'type' field set to 'content' and a 'content' field containing the actual prediction\". Using `.get(\"type\") == \"content\"` on a non-dict would raise AttributeError, so guard with isinstance. Upstream's guard lines quoted: `if \"prediction\" in kwargs:`, `and kwargs[\"prediction\"].get(\"type\") == \"content\"`, `and \"content\" in kwargs[\"prediction\"]`. The WebFetch summary mentioned \"if a `\"prediction\"` kwarg was passed and it is a dict whose...\" — so there's an isinstance check too. I'll include it.\n\nLet me make the edit.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Z.AI","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"daf4bd95-90eb-4bd5-a8b2-615c44312155","uuid":"358266ed-4031-4a71-9633-3940606e73f3","timestamp":"2026-09-03T17:46:19.487Z","request_id":"gen-1788457551-waGDypCl7yIdFjBjMCnL"}
