{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788457627-I72SPqW0oaOtjFoAXsvt","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So dicts get JSON-dumped with double quotes: `{\"type\": \"unsupported_type\", \"content\": \"...\"}` while my assertion uses `str(prediction)` which produces single quotes: `{'type': 'unsupported_type', ...}`. That would fail for dicts. But the string values inside (`\"unsupported_type\"`, `\"The capital of France is Paris.\"`) would appear in the JSON dump.\n\nSimplest fix: assert on a distinctive substring rather than the whole `str(prediction)`. For each parametrized value, use the content or a distinctive marker:\n- `\"The capital of France is Paris.\"` → appears in JSON dump.\n- `{\"type\": \"unsupported_type\", ...}` → `unsupported_type` appears.\n- `{\"type\": \"content\"}` → `{'type': 'content'}` JSON dumps to `{\"type\": \"content\"}` — substring `\"content\"` appears, but that's too generic... Actually it's still fine as a check that it rendered, but weak. Better: parametrize with (prediction, expected_substring) pairs.\n\nLet me rewrite that test with explicit expected substrings. For `{\"type\": \"content\"}` the JSON dump is `{\"type\": \"content\"}` so expected substring could be `{\"type\": \"content\"}` (with double quotes as JSON). Hmm, `json.dumps({\"type\": \"content\"})` → `{\"type\": \"content\"}`. Yes exactly.\n\nFor the string value `\"The capital of France is Paris.\"` → `serialize_for_json` on a str returns the str, which is not dict/list → `str(...)` → unchanged. Good.\n\nFor the dict `{\"type\": \"unsupported_type\", \"content\": \"...\"}` → JSON dump with `\", \"` separators: `{\"type\": \"unsupported_type\", \"content\": \"The capital of France is Paris.\"}`. So expected substring: `\"unsupported_type\"`.\n\nAlso the typed annotation: I declared `prediction: str = dspy.InputField()` but passing dicts. There's no runtime type validation in this codebase version (upstream added `warn_on_type_mismatch` later), so it's fine, but to be cleaner I could leave the annotation off. Let me keep the signature simple: `prediction = dspy.InputField()` without annotation — avoids implying a type contract that the test violates.\n\nWait — does DSPy's signature require annotations? No, unannotated fields are allowed (default Any).\n\nAlso note the last message assertion: `lm.calls[0][\"messages\"][-1][\"content\"]` is the user message containing all input fields. Good.\n\nLet me rewrite the malformed test.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Z.AI","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"daf4bd95-90eb-4bd5-a8b2-615c44312155","uuid":"a2c765f1-3680-444b-b839-7124f19b4f57","timestamp":"2026-09-03T17:47:37.658Z","request_id":"gen-1788457627-I72SPqW0oaOtjFoAXsvt"}
